#!/bin/sh
# gh-analyst — run `gh` as the analyst identity: a GitHub App, on the supervisor box.
#
# THE FOURTH WRAPPER, AND THE FIRST WHOSE SEAT BOTH AUTHORS AND APPROVES. `gh-bot` and `gh-coder`
# are the implementer, `gh-review` is the architect. Those two seats are deliberately disjoint: one
# writes, the other judges, and `approver ≠ author` is a property of which box holds which key. This
# seat is neither of them. (The FIFTH until quince#676 retired `bin/gh-arch`, the architect's second
# wrapper — the seat count is unchanged, the wrapper count is not.)
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. The supervisor box is where the Operator and a session talk — it studies the
# system, files what does not match, and works a problem space into specified work items. It had NO
# forge credential at all, so everything it produced went out borrowed: quince#237-#246, quince#344,
# quince-devlog#139 and #141 were all authored as `app/quince-coder` with a hand-written relay banner
# saying who really wrote them. quince#344 is the proposal arguing that borrowing produces a false
# record, and it was itself filed on a borrowed identity. This wrapper is what stops that.
#
# THE GRANT, ruled 2026-07-31 on quince#375: `contents: write` + `pull_requests: write` +
# `issues: write` + `metadata: read`, installed on `quince`, `quince-devlog`, `ios-backup-crypt`,
# `ios-backup-parser` and the private `quince-local`. MEASURED rather than read off a settings page
# (quince#375, the ceremony report), including one measured NEGATIVE: a write under
# `.github/workflows/**` returns 403 while an ordinary write to the same branch succeeds seconds
# later. So quince#113's rule holds for a third identity — no agent seat can push a workflow.
#
# WHY THE WIDE GRANT IS NOT A WIDENING, since a reader meeting this file first will ask. The
# supervisor box already holds root ssh into both working seats, and root ssh to a box that holds a
# credential IS that credential. `contents: write` adds no REACHABLE capability; it removes the
# reason to borrow one. Per-repo narrowing was ruled out as theatre for the same reason.
#
# Same allowlist reason as the other four: a permission rule never matches past a leading
# `VAR=value`, so `GH_TOKEN=$(…) gh …` is unallowlistable by construction. Allowlist the script.
set -eu

# HOME IS NOT GUARANTEED, AND `set -u` MAKES A BARE $HOME FATAL. Resolved from the passwd database
# rather than assumed, so the same invocation works from a shell, from an OpenRC service (which gets
# no HOME, and that is what took the arch box down after a reboot) and from cron.
_home="${HOME:-$(awk -F: -v u="$(id -u)" '$3==u{print $6; exit}' /etc/passwd 2>/dev/null || true)}"
[ -n "$_home" ] || {
	printf 'gh-analyst: cannot locate a home directory — HOME is unset and uid %s has no passwd entry.\n' "$(id -u)" >&2
	printf 'gh-analyst: set QUINCE_ANALYST_KEY_FILE and QUINCE_ANALYST_APP_ID_FILE explicitly.\n' >&2
	exit 1
}

KEY_FILE="${QUINCE_ANALYST_KEY_FILE:-$_home/.config/quince/quince-analyst.pem}"
APP_ID_FILE="${QUINCE_ANALYST_APP_ID_FILE:-$_home/.config/quince/quince-analyst.app-id}"
API="${QUINCE_GITHUB_API:-https://api.github.com}"
AH="Accept: application/vnd.github+json"

die() {
	printf 'gh-analyst: %s\n' "$1" >&2
	shift
	for line in "$@"; do printf 'gh-analyst: %s\n' "$line" >&2; done
	exit 1
}

# IDENTITY BEFORE DEPENDENCIES (quince#157), AND THIS SEAT REFUSES BESIDE ALL FOUR.
#
# The other wrappers each refuse beside ONE SIDE of the boundary: `gh-review` refuses the implementer
# identity because it approves; `gh-coder` and `gh-bot` refuse the approving identities because they
# author. Every one of them can name the half it is not.
#
# This seat can do BOTH — it authors pull requests and it can cast a verdict on somebody else's — so
# there is no half to name. What keeps it honest is not a separation of powers within one box; it is
# that this box holds exactly one identity, so everything it does is attributable to the seat that
# did it. `approver ≠ author` survives per-PR, because GitHub refuses an approval from a PR's own
# author, and `.github/CODEOWNERS` routes canon to `@novkostya`, which no App can satisfy.
#
# So the rule is the strongest of the five and the simplest to state: ANY other seat's credential
# here is a refusal.
#
# That mirrors `deploy/runner/preflight`'s supervisor arm, which is an allowlist of exactly this one
# file. Two checks, same claim, from opposite ends: `preflight` gates whether the box STARTS, this
# gates every action it takes, so a hand run cannot quietly cross a boundary the service refuses to.
#
# THIS SENTENCE WAS FALSE FOR ONE REVIEW CYCLE AND THE SEQUENCE IS WORTH KEEPING (quince#377 review).
# The allowlist is PR 1c and landed as quince#380; this line asserted it while the arm was still a
# denylist that did not know this identity existed. What made that worth a blocking finding rather
# than tense-pedantry: the CEREMONY PLACED THE KEY BEFORE THE MECHANISM LANDED, so on the supervisor
# box `preflight` was scanning four paths, finding none, and printing *"no forge credential present —
# this seat can neither author nor approve, as required"* — false about a box that held an authoring
# credential, from a gate whose design is to refuse rather than degrade.
#
# The claim is true again now, and it is true because quince#380 merged rather than because the
# wording was softened. Recorded because the window is the interesting artefact: a comment describing
# a mechanism that does not exist yet, in the file a session reads to find out whether it does, is the
# same defect as one describing a mechanism that has moved — and this one closed in both directions
# inside a day.
#
# Ordered above the dependency loop for the reason quince#157 records: an environment refusal invites
# a repair, and a session repairing its way past this one ends up holding two identities on one box.
# A boundary refusal must outrank an environment refusal because the environment is meant to be fixed
# and the boundary is not. It needs nothing from the loop — `$_home` is resolved and `[ -f ]` is a
# builtin — so ordering it first costs nothing.
#
# THE LIVE CREDENTIALS ARE NAMED FIRST, for the reason quince#204 pinned in the other wrappers: a
# refusal that names a RETIRED credential sends a session to delete it, watch the refusal persist,
# and conclude the guard is broken. `quince-bot.token` is suspended and `decisions/0014` condition 1
# requires it be left wherever it lives, so it is checked last.
REVIEW_KEY="${QUINCE_REVIEW_KEY_FILE:-$_home/.config/quince/quince-review.pem}"
if [ -f "$REVIEW_KEY" ]; then
	die "a REVIEWER APP KEY is present at $REVIEW_KEY." \
		"This seat authors pull requests; holding the reviewer identity too would let one box write a" \
		"change and cast the architect's verdict on it. Remove it, or run gh-review on the reviewer box."
fi
CODER_KEY="${QUINCE_CODER_KEY_FILE:-$_home/.config/quince/quince-coder.pem}"
if [ -f "$CODER_KEY" ]; then
	die "an IMPLEMENTER APP KEY is present at $CODER_KEY." \
		"This seat can approve; holding the implementer identity too would let one box author work" \
		"with gh-coder and approve it with this wrapper. Remove it, or run gh-coder on the implementer box."
fi
ARCH_TOKEN="${QUINCE_ARCH_TOKEN_FILE:-$_home/.config/quince/quince-arch.token}"
if [ -f "$ARCH_TOKEN" ]; then
	die "an ARCHITECT CREDENTIAL is present at $ARCH_TOKEN." \
		"That seat merges. A box that can author and merge is the whole of what the seat separation" \
		"exists to prevent. REMOVE IT: that credential is retired and no wrapper reads it any more" \
		"(quince#676), so its presence here is a leftover file rather than a seat in the wrong place."
fi
BOT_TOKEN="${QUINCE_BOT_TOKEN_FILE:-$_home/.config/quince/quince-bot.token}"
if [ -f "$BOT_TOKEN" ]; then
	die "a BOT TOKEN is present at $BOT_TOKEN." \
		"That is the legacy implementer identity (decisions/0014). It is suspended, but a boundary" \
		"check is a claim about THE BOX and not about whether a credential currently authenticates" \
		"(quince#232). Remove it, or run gh-bot on the implementer box."
fi


# A CREDENTIAL IS NOT A SET OF PATHNAMES (quince#385). Every check above names a FILE, and a forge
# credential in the ENVIRONMENT crosses all of them without one of them speaking — `GH_TOKEN` is
# exactly how this wrapper hands a token to `gh` fifty lines below, so a value already sitting there
# is a working credential belonging to somebody else.
#
# `deploy/runner/preflight`'s supervisor arm learned this by passing a box with `GH_TOKEN=ghp_…` set,
# and states the rule in as many words. The wrappers did not carry it — and the asymmetry ran the
# wrong way, because `preflight` gates whether the box STARTS while this gates EVERY ACTION it takes.
#
# DUPLICATED RATHER THAN SOURCED, following quince#316's ruling rather than re-litigating it: a
# shared block would put a load-time file dependency on the security path of a credential wrapper,
# where a missing or tampered file is a new failure mode. `bin/wrapper-boundary-test` carries the
# detector that fails when a wrapper is added without this, which is the same trade that ruling made.
for _envvar in GH_TOKEN GITHUB_TOKEN GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN; do
	eval "_envval=\${$_envvar:-}"
	[ -n "$_envval" ] || continue
	die "a FORGE CREDENTIAL is present in the environment: \$$_envvar." \
		"That variable is how this wrapper hands a token to gh, so a value already in it is somebody" \
		"else's working credential and would be used ahead of this seat's identity." \
		"Unset it, and run this wrapper on a box that holds one identity."
done
if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then
	printf 'usage: gh-analyst <gh arguments...>\n' >&2
	printf '  runs gh as the quince-analyst App, minting a fresh installation token per call\n' >&2
	printf '  key %s\n  app id %s\n' "$KEY_FILE" "$APP_ID_FILE" >&2
	exit 2
fi

for tool in gh openssl curl jq; do
	command -v "$tool" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "$tool is not installed" \
		"this wrapper needs gh, openssl, curl and jq. On Alpine: apk add openssl curl jq." \
		"openssl in particular is NOT in a minimal Alpine image and RS256 signing cannot proceed without it."
done

[ -f "$KEY_FILE" ] || die "no App private key at $KEY_FILE" \
	"Refusing to fall back to the ambient gh credentials: doing so would act as whoever is signed in," \
	"which on the Operator's own machine means acting as the Operator — the exact confusion quince#47" \
	"records and quince-devlog#118 was caught committing, and the confusion this identity exists to end." \
	"Generate a key from the App's settings page and place it there, mode 600."
[ -f "$APP_ID_FILE" ] || die "no App id at $APP_ID_FILE" \
	"The id is not a secret — it is on the App's settings page — but it is environment-specific," \
	"so it is configuration and not a constant in a public repository."

APP_ID=$(cat "$APP_ID_FILE")

# --- mint a JWT, then exchange it for an installation token ----------------------------------
# NOT CACHED, deliberately, and for the same reasons as the other App wrappers: an installation token
# lives one hour, so a cache would be a second secret at rest with a lifetime this wrapper would have
# to manage, plus an invalidation bug waiting to happen. Two HTTPS round-trips per call is the price.
b64url() { openssl base64 -A | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '='; }

now=$(date +%s)
header=$(printf '{"alg":"RS256","typ":"JWT"}' | b64url)
# 60s of backdate absorbs clock skew between this box and GitHub; 9 minutes of life is well inside
# the 10-minute maximum GitHub accepts for an App JWT.
payload=$(printf '{"iat":%d,"exp":%d,"iss":"%s"}' "$((now - 60))" "$((now + 540))" "$APP_ID" | b64url)
signature=$(printf '%s.%s' "$header" "$payload" | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary -sign "$KEY_FILE" | b64url)
jwt="$header.$payload.$signature"

installations=$(curl -sS --fail-with-body -H "Authorization: Bearer $jwt" -H "$AH" \
	"$API/app/installations" 2>&1) || die "could not list installations" \
	"$(printf '%s' "$installations" | head -n 3)" \
	"Most likely: the key does not match app id $APP_ID, or the key has been revoked."

# FIVE REPOSITORIES IS ONE INSTALLATION, and the distinction is worth a line because the ruling
# enumerates five repos and this branch counts something else. An App installed on five repositories
# of ONE account is a single installation with five repositories selected; the count here is
# installations per ACCOUNT. So the expected answer is 1, and a 2 would mean the App had been
# installed somewhere nobody ruled — which is exactly the state the override exists to make explicit
# rather than silently pick a side of.
count=$(printf '%s' "$installations" | jq -r 'if type=="array" then length else 0 end')
case "$count" in
0) die "app id $APP_ID has no installations" \
	"The App exists but is installed nowhere, so it can reach no repository. Install it on the" \
	"account and select the repositories ruled on quince#375." ;;
1) : ;;
*) # Picking .[0] would work today and silently pick the wrong one later. A wrapper that guesses
	# which identity it is acting as is the thing this file exists to prevent.
	[ -n "${QUINCE_ANALYST_INSTALLATION_ID:-}" ] || die \
		"app id $APP_ID has $count installations; refusing to guess which one to act as." \
		"Set QUINCE_ANALYST_INSTALLATION_ID to the one this box should use." ;;
esac

install_id="${QUINCE_ANALYST_INSTALLATION_ID:-$(printf '%s' "$installations" | jq -r '.[0].id')}"

token=$(curl -sS --fail-with-body -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $jwt" -H "$AH" \
	"$API/app/installations/$install_id/access_tokens" 2>&1) || die \
	"could not mint an installation token for installation $install_id" \
	"$(printf '%s' "$token" | head -n 3)"

GH_TOKEN=$(printf '%s' "$token" | jq -r '.token // empty')
[ -n "$GH_TOKEN" ] || die "installation $install_id returned no token" \
	"Refusing to continue: an empty token would fall through to the ambient credentials."

unset GITHUB_TOKEN
export GH_TOKEN

# SELF-CAUSED LEDGER (quince#242). Two verbs — `pr review` and `pr merge` — produce forge events that
# carry NO actor, because `forge-watch` computes them by diffing observations. Recording them as this
# runner performs them is what lets its own watch tell them from another seat's.
#
# THIS WRAPPER IS WHY THE LEDGER LOOP IN `bin/wrapper-boundary-test` EXISTS. quince#316 declined a
# refactor that would have shared one block across the wrappers, on the measurement that the fallback
# path was almost never taken — and named its revisit trigger as *a fourth wrapper appearing*. That
# suite's last case is the detector it settled on instead. This is the fifth wrapper, and the four
# lines below are what it is checking for.
#
# THE `exec` IS KEPT FOR EVERYTHING ELSE, and that is the whole reason `classify` exists as a
# separate verb: this is a CREDENTIAL wrapper, so the blast radius of a behaviour change here is
# every forge call the session makes. Only the two recordable verbs take the fork-and-wait path.
#
# RECORDED ONLY ON SUCCESS. A `gh pr review` that failed produces no event to suppress, so its row
# would sit unconsumed until it swallowed the NEXT verdict on that PR — somebody else's, which is a
# MISSED wake and the one direction this subsystem must not fail in. The exit status is passed
# through unchanged either way; the ledger never affects what the caller sees.
_ledger="$(dirname "$0")/forge-ledger"
if [ -x "$_ledger" ] && "$_ledger" classify "$@" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	gh "$@" && _rc=0 || _rc=$?
	if [ "$_rc" -eq 0 ]; then "$_ledger" record "$@" || :; fi
	exit "$_rc"
fi

exec gh "$@"
